Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
everyone it's tomorrow i am here in  sony's offices and i am one of the first  people outside of sony to get my hands  on the playstation vr2 and i also just  played horizon call of the mountain  build by firestrike studios um first  things first the actual device the  playstation vr to it seems like it's  come a long way since the original  device i think the the most immediate  thing that you'll notice is the  comfortability um i put it on and pretty  much immediately there were a number of  options for me to get it onto my face  and in place  in the optimal way there's buttons on  the back buttons on the front um it now  allows you to adjust the  distance between the two lenses so you  can have it pretty much perfectly for  you um everything that you expect from  it still works you know if you want  glasses on glasses off  and uh the the big big ticket of of  course are the new um controllers which  are  based somewhat on the dualsense um and  they obviously take a lot of um  modernization and a lot of evolutions  from uh controller technology in the vr  space specifically the oculus stuff the  whole finger tracking stuff is something  that has been pretty much associated  with oculus's quests and riffs and that  kind of stuff and now it is a version of  it for the playstation vr2 and it works  really really well i know that because i  played horizon call of the mountain  which obviously if you played horizon  you will know involves a lot of bow and  arrow shooting but in this game in this  version of the game there's also a lot  of climbing um so which meant that i was  able to really check the fidelity of how  it feels to use your hands and whether  that's climbing up the up things like  ladders or scaling the side of mountains  or even doing things like picking up  apples or lids or barrels and kind of  really inspecting it um horizon call it  a mountain very impressive um i had one  of those moments where i was like oh  this is why vr is magical and it's worth  pursuing um start off on a boat ride  obviously you're playing a new character  for the this game specifically and you  begin with a classic vr  moment where you're sitting in a bowl  and you're kind of going through  an area from the game and it was pretty  incredible seeing  the world of horizon from that  perspective first person perspective it  would be enough but this is a immersed  vr first person perspective where you  can see all like the shrubbery and and  the bright colors and the mountains and  the and the brilliant blue sky and the  the blinding light of the sun and then  you get to see the various robot animals  kind of come and go and the sense of  scale is very very very overwhelming at  times um  i described it as having a jurassic park  moment where you know the sense of  wonder that you get in that you see in  that movie where those characters are  seeing dinosaurs you know wandering by  it was very very similar where you know  we had these these enemies that i'd been  seeing my entire life you've been  playing that franchise from the end of a  bow and arrow now up close and personal  and it was an impressive fidelity pretty  much everywhere that i looked there was  a high level of detail and and it was  very very impressive um the other thing  that i really liked about that um demo  that i played for horizon called the  mountain was it felt like an  amalgamation of a lot of the the  singular vr experiences that you would  have up until this point you had games  that were you know focused on oh you're  a tourist in this world so you kind of  just sit down and watch and get to enjoy  the environments or you play another one  which is like oh this is a bow and arrow  game where you a lot of the gameplay  elements and the gameplay design is  focused on you either shooting a gun or  firing a bow and arrow and there's all  this is a climbing game where the only  thing you really do is climb just put  all that together and put it in the wall  of horizon in a way that made a lot of  sense um so in any in my across my  entire playtime i went from simply  walking which is done by holding two  buns and then moving your hands up and  down which was felt quite intuitive and  it kind of  i felt it to be a good middle ground  between the previous two um  movement  methods that we're used to which is like  teleportation or like actively pressing  a stick to move forward i felt like i  had a little more control and i felt a  little more kind of involved in the  process which also helped reduce the  kind of sense of motion sickness that  people might um feel you know mileage  will still vary but as someone who is  motion sick i didn't feel myself  immediately looking to hurl up um my  breakfast  but i did enjoy that kind of feeling of  moving around so i walked around a bit i  got to inspect the environments and then  went from there and went into climbing  the climbing part of it very intuitive  simply holding down the triggers as i  was putting my hand in place and it was  almo it would almost snap to the  grabable ledges and then i was just  physically pulling myself up and it was  so intuitive that i kind of like really  got immersed in the the feeling of and  the process of like looking out for  handholds and then grabbing them and  then by the time i realized i was way  way high up a mountain and i did the  instinctive thing which was looking down  and  that overwhelming sense of peril that  you get from vr sometimes kind of came  over me so i had to really like tell  myself oh this will be fine don't worry  about it and i think that really speaks  to like the fidelity of what they've um  kind of rendered with this is like a  very beautiful very high res a very  detailed game so i i found that to be  quite impressive and then we went on to  some scenarios which are a little more  combat focused um the weapon i had was a  bow and arrow um picked up you can  choose what hand you want to kind of  wield it with and it does the classic vr  thing where you put it behind your back  and you can also like pull out um arrows  to use together and then it's manual  aiming there wasn't a reticle on site so  it was simply a case of like eyeballing  it and doing and letting your arrows fly  based on that which felt good it felt  like that's where like a lot of the  skills gonna come for um interestingly  obviously you cannot do a dodge roll in  this game because you will throw up so  the the kind of system that they've come  up with is you hold two buttons and kind  of like  swing your arms and then let go at the  end of it and your character will do a  hop in that direction there's pretty  much free movement up until combat major  combat scenarios so when you're walking  around the world you can look around in  kind of increments and then use the  movement method to move back and forth  however when you're in a big boss fight  you have almost like a path set out for  you so you'll only be able to dodge in  certain um along these paths you can't  like run forward and attack or run back  and attack or dodge and that kind of  stuff so it kind of is a little more  fixated to make sure that you're not  over overdoing it in terms of movement  um and then i also fought a bunch of  different scale of enemies which was the  impressive thing at one point in the in  the beginning you get to kind of see the  tallness walk by and it was it is very  very impressive to be underneath it and  look up and see that that that character  model in scale and it did make me think  i will be able to climb up to the top of  that and also um kind of like see the  world from the top down i spoke to the  developers fire spray and and um gorilla  afterwards and they talked about the  whole campaign lasting roughly seven  hours if you beeline it i don't expect  people will be lying at first time  through and there is a  lot of value in stopping to like really  suck in the scenery because as as i  mentioned playstation vr2 is a big big  step up based on the little i've played  so far from the original especially when  it comes to fidelity so you will want to  smell the virtual roses and pick arrows  out of enemies and kind of figure out  what  what how did what makes this world tick  so and and i found that rewarding if i  had more time i probably would have  spent way more time just walking around  and looking at stuff but based on the  little that i played i was very very  impressed one with call of the mountain  and two with the headset  i'm excited to get more time with the  headset and also that controller and  hopefully i'll be able to do that again  soon  thanks for watching keep it on gamespot  for more on horizon and playstation vr2  hey  shadow karja you can run but i will find  you  i'm not trying to run i'm  hey  so she's alive  and she thinks i'm trying to escape  hey

